<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eastern Bloc</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1947">1947</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Communist Bloc, Soviet Bloc, Socialist Bloc</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eastern Bloc, also called the Communist Bloc or Soviet Bloc, was an unofficial coalition of communist states aligned with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, from 1947 to 1991, and opposed to the capitalist Western Bloc. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> it meant the Soviet Union and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> states; in Asia it included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> from 1961. Soviet control was tested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-revolution-of-1956">Hungarian Revolution of 1956</a>, which the Soviet Union suppressed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a> of 1968, ended by invasion. The bloc disintegrated after the Polish election of June 1989 and the Pan-European Picnic of 19 August 1989.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A member of the <b>Eastern Bloc</b>, North Vietnam was recognised by almost all communist countries, China first, and received their aid; many non-aligned states gave it de facto recognition, and its clandestine weapon transfers during the Algerian War made relations with Algeria close.</blockquote><a href="/empire/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Yugoslavia's break with Stalin in 1948 was the first open rupture in the <b>Eastern Bloc</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The de-Stalinisation of Czechoslovakia began under Antonín Novotný in the late 1950s and early 1960s but progressed more slowly than in most other states of the <b>Eastern Bloc</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/prague-spring">Prague Spring · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The survival of Solidarity had no precedent in Poland or in the whole <b>Eastern Bloc</b>, a break in the hard-line stance of the ruling party and of the Soviet government.</blockquote><a href="/movement/solidarity">Solidarity · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 1970s and 1980s the economies of the <b>Eastern Bloc</b> had stagnated and fallen further behind Western Europe, a decline that helped bring the system down in 1989–1991.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Derek R. Ford, by contrast, emphasises its continuity with Marxism–Leninism and credits it as the principle that let North Korea survive the collapse of the <b>Eastern Bloc</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/juche">Juche · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
